"""
The first board's last layer, its budget a row at a time.

pta_board_chain.py ran a stand-in for the GRXGPU charter's first-board
classifier with its last layer on the tile, and three files after it moved
that layer's error by a quarter at the most: a range fitted
(pta_board_range.py), a ninth bit (pta_board_bits.py), a source lit
(pta_board_lit.py).  None of them said what the error is made of.  Against
its declared format a tile at this plan's version 2 (B14) is off by its
converter's rounding and by four rows of the budget:

    the receiver   its noise, 0.25 of an LSB of the 8-bit converter
    the shot       the shot noise of 30 photons an LSB
    programming    a weight off by an LSB of an 8-bit weight, rms
    crosstalk      2% of each neighbouring row's weight

This is each of the four alone over the format, each left out of the tile as
budgeted, and the shot noise and the crosstalk left out together; and all of
it again with the source lit as pta_board_lit.py lit it (B16, its lines read
and corrected on the weights), since what a source adds was found there to
depend on what it is added to.

MEASURED IN A MODEL, by grx930 (c930/doc/pta_error_model_design_note.md
section 5, "The first board's last layer, its budget a row at a time",
2026-10-13; `SHAPE=chain sim/pta_mnist.sh DIR WORK chainrows`).
pta_board_chain.py's networks, tile and draws, 10,000 images, twenty-two
rows: eleven with no source and the same eleven lit.  Four of them are runs
the files before this one hold, line for line: the format alone and the tile
as budgeted are pta_board_chain.py's, and the two of them lit are
pta_board_lit.py's.  The harness is not changed: every row is one it could
already run.

A row with one row of the budget alone is read over the format alone; a row
with one left out, over the tile as budgeted; a lit row, over its own row
with no source; each of its draw.

THE PROBE'S GAIN, which this file is the first to hold.  The harness's probe
measures a run's error against the host's own network, weights at the
network's width and activations as the host has them, and not against the
declared format.  Of that error it fits a gain: the slope of the error on the
outputs themselves, over the run, with each image's common shift taken out
first.  A positive gain is a last layer whose every output is that fraction
too large.  And it gives what is left with the shift and the gain out.  A
class does not move with a shift or with a gain.  The declared format has a
gain of its own against the host's network, so a row's gain is read here
against the format alone's.

WHAT THE MODEL'S CROSSTALK IS.  An input's light also passes the two
neighbouring rows' rings, so a row's weight is its own and chi times each
neighbour's, chi being 5/256.  Summed over a column that is the column's own
weights times the neighbours' activations, times chi.  Were every activation
the same, it would be the sum itself times twice chi: a gain of 3.9%.

DERIVED here:

  a row's mean and   which have to be the nine tables grx930 printed, cell
  its error          for cell.  The four rows of the files before this one
                     are worked out from those files' figures
  what a row adds    to another, squares for squares: the root of the mean,
                     over its 25 runs, of its rms squared less the other's
                     of the same network and draw.  It is what the row would
                     add if its error and the other's were independent
  the rows together  the root of the squares of the format alone and of what
                     each of the four adds to it: what the tile as budgeted
                     would be off by if the five were independent
  a row's gain       its probe's gain less another row's, run for run: the
                     format alone's of its draw unless one is named

HOW SURE.  Five networks, 2.8 errors for one in twenty.  The format and the
crosstalk are not drawn: with no source a row of those two alone is one run
whatever its draw.

WHAT THIS IS NOT.  grxgpu's classifier, or a chip: everything
pta_board_chain.py says of itself.  A device's crosstalk: the model's is the
same fraction of each neighbour in every column, and a chip's is whatever its
layout makes it.  A gain against the declared format: the probe's is against
the host's network.  A tile with its gain calibrated out: none was run.  A
remedy.

Standard library only.  Run:  python3 docs/designs/pta_board_budget.py
"""
import contextlib
import io
import math
import os
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()):
    import pta_board_chain as chain
    import pta_board_lit as lit

MNIST, FASHION, INVERTED = chain.MNIST, chain.FASHION, chain.INVERTED
WORKLOADS, KINDS = chain.WORKLOADS, chain.KINDS
BEFORE, REFERENCE = chain.BEFORE, chain.REFERENCE
SEEDS, DRAWS, PLAN, T95 = chain.SEEDS, chain.DRAWS, chain.PLAN, chain.T95
ALL = chain.ALL                                # the test set
TOP, OUTPUTS = chain.TOP, chain.OUTPUTS
GATE_RMS, GATE_MAX, GATE_CLASS = chain.GATE_RMS, chain.GATE_MAX, chain.GATE_CLASS
HOST, DECLARED_CLASS = chain.HOST, chain.DECLARED_CLASS
DARK, LIT = lit.TRAIN, lit.LIT                 # no source; and B16's, its lines read and corrected on the weights
# version 2's four rows (B14) as the harness is given them: the receiver's noise in LSB of the 8-bit
# converter, photons an LSB, a programming error in LSB of an 8-bit weight, and the crosstalk
BUDGET = {"thermal": 0.25, "shot": 30, "prog": 1, "xtalk": 0.02}
FOUR = tuple(BUDGET)
CHI = round(256 * BUDGET["xtalk"]) / 256        # the crosstalk as the tile holds it: in 256ths
# a row's core -> the rows of the budget in it
IN = {"format": (), "thermal": ("thermal",), "shot": ("shot",), "prog": ("prog",), "xtalk": ("xtalk",),
      "less_thermal": ("shot", "prog", "xtalk"), "less_shot": ("thermal", "prog", "xtalk"),
      "less_prog": ("thermal", "shot", "xtalk"), "less_xtalk": ("thermal", "shot", "prog"),
      "less_two": ("thermal", "prog"), "budget": FOUR}
CORES = tuple(IN)
ROWS = tuple((core, light) for light in (DARK, LIT) for core in CORES)
# the four rows the files before this one hold: pta_board_chain.py's two, and pta_board_lit.py's
THEIRS = (("format", DARK), ("budget", DARK), ("format", LIT), ("budget", LIT))
NAME = {"format": "the format alone", "thermal": "the receiver's noise alone", "shot": "the shot noise alone",
        "prog": "the programming error alone", "xtalk": "the crosstalk alone", "less_thermal": "less the receiver's noise",
        "less_shot": "less the shot noise", "less_prog": "less the programming error", "less_xtalk": "less the crosstalk",
        "less_two": "less the shot noise and the crosstalk", "budget": "as budgeted"}
assert len(ROWS) == 22 and TOP == 127 and DRAWS == (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) and CHI == 5 / 256
assert all(set(IN["less_" + c]) == set(FOUR) - {c} for c in FOUR) and set(IN["less_two"]) == set(FOUR) - {"shot", "xtalk"}

